+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=4.\n$$\n\nAnd from part 3, we know that the gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇f(1,1)=∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=⟨4,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =4+4(x-1)+2(y-1) \\\\  & =4x+2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366304 13194139535788 true] [17592186366303 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535788 true] [17592186366303 1041 "field-1" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366303 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366304 13194139535788 true] [17592186366303 1110 #uuid "014a9864-05da-5c39-abda-053122121538" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366304 1034 "4x+2y-2" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366304 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535788 true] [17592186366304 1111 #uuid "f9509931-6069-5071-924a-07b226a79a62" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366305 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366306 13194139535788 true] [17592186366305 1089 "q-168118" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366305 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535788 true] [17592186366305 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069903/assets/4fce9c5eebc7b439d9851ed0ed20df2e0879161b754d63fcd31502b26a8fb82e.png)\n\n \n\nA thin layer of fluid flows over a certain region of the plane, as shown above. The corresponding velocity vector field is given by $V(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+2xy\\,j.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nThe divergence $\\text{div}\\,V$ of the velocity field represents the scalar rate of expansion of the fluid per unit of area at a given point. Find the corresponding expansion rate at the point $(1,1).$\