       \n                            \n                                        $4x+2y-2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe scalar curl of a plane velocity field is called the **vorticity,** denoted $\\omega .$ The vorticity describes a measure of the local rotation of the field at a given point. Find the vorticity of $V$ at $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\omega (1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139535788 true] [17592186366305 1113 #uuid "4eda8491-df5c-5e0e-9aa7-cb47bdba2401" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366305 1145 "If $V=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $V$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $V$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $P={x}^{2}y$ and $Q=2xy.$ Therefore, the scalar curl (i.e., the vorticity) of $V$ at $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega  & =\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega (1,1) & =(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366306 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366307 13194139535788 true] [17592186366306 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535788 true] [17592186366306 1041 "field-1" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366306 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366307 13194139535788 true] [17592186366306 1110 #uuid "cb77af87-7281-5edc-8473-ef66c97683d8" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366307 1034 "1" 13194139535788 true] [17592186366307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535788 true] [17592186366307 1111 #uuid "42e99af7-635c-5472-a055-9f84977504e8" 13194139535788 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-168113" 17592186366286 "q-168113/field-1" 17592186366287 "q-168113/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" 17592186366288 "q-168114" 17592186366289 "q-168114/field-1" 17592186366290 "q-168114/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" 17592186366291 "q-168114/field-2" 17592186366292 "q-168114/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" 17592186366293 "q-168115" 17592186366294 "q-168115/field-1" 17592186366295 "q-168115/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" 17592186366296 "q-168115/field-2" 17592186366297 "q-168115/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" 17592186366298 "q-168116" 17592186366299 "q-168116/field-1" 17592186366300 "q-168116/field-1/1f5b353af7cbb90a89e4a07f196cff5b0ba27d9934d00e9a38de61eff80108f7" 17592186366301 "q-168117" 17592186366302 "q-168117/field-1" 17592186366303 "q-168117/field-1/b13fc961e08923649e55f2fcca8a8088a2c382ef7d0f4e1a87c5e648a3a4a76f" 17592186366304 "q-1